entries.wine_behaviour:
Does no work at all, fabricates a registration handle, and reports success:

NTSTATUS WINAPI ObRegisterCallbacks(POB_CALLBACK_REGISTRATION callback, void **handle)
{
    FIXME( "callback %p, handle %p.\n", callback, handle );

    if(handle)
        *handle = UlongToHandle(0xdeadbeaf);

    return STATUS_SUCCESS;
}

The POB_CALLBACK_REGISTRATION is never read, never stored, and no callback is ever invoked. 0xdeadbeaf is a literal. ObUnRegisterCallbacks (ntoskrnl.c:3165) accepts the fabricated value and is a bare FIXME.

void WINAPI ObUnRegisterCallbacks(void *handle)
{
    FIXME( "stub: %p\n", handle );
}

No work; it silently accepts the 0xdeadbeaf value its partner fabricated.

USHORT WINAPI ObGetFilterVersion(void)
{
    FIXME( "stub:\n" );

    return OB_FLT_REGISTRATION_VERSION;
}

OB_FLT_REGISTRATION_VERSION is 0x0100 (include/ddk/wdm.h:1619-1620). Marked "stub" yet it returns the affirmative value.

NTSTATUS WINAPI PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutine( PCREATE_PROCESS_NOTIFY_ROUTINE callback, BOOLEAN remove )
{
    FIXME( "stub: %p %d\n", callback, remove );
    return STATUS_SUCCESS;
}

The callback pointer is discarded. Nothing is stored, nothing is ever called, and both the register and the remove direction claim success.

NTSTATUS WINAPI PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutineEx( PCREATE_PROCESS_NOTIFY_ROUTINE_EX callback, BOOLEAN remove )
{
    FIXME( "stub: %p %d\n", callback, remove );
    return STATUS_SUCCESS;
}

Identical no-op-plus-success to the non-Ex form.

NTSTATUS WINAPI PsSetCreateThreadNotifyRoutine( PCREATE_THREAD_NOTIFY_ROUTINE NotifyRoutine )
{
    FIXME( "stub: %p\n", NotifyRoutine );
    return STATUS_SUCCESS;
}

Pointer discarded, success reported.

NTSTATUS WINAPI PsRemoveCreateThreadNotifyRoutine( PCREATE_THREAD_NOTIFY_ROUTINE NotifyRoutine )
{
    FIXME( "stub: %p\n", NotifyRoutine );
    return STATUS_SUCCESS;
}

Doubly deceptive: it confirms removal of a routine that was never registered in the first place.

NTSTATUS WINAPI PsSetLoadImageNotifyRoutine(PLOAD_IMAGE_NOTIFY_ROUTINE routine)
{
    return PsSetLoadImageNotifyRoutineEx(routine, 0);
}

A genuine tail-call into a routine that genuinely registers. Not deceptive.

Actually registers, and honestly labels itself "semi-stub":

    FIXME("routine %p, flags %Ix semi-stub.\n", routine, flags);

    if (load_image_notify_routine_count == ARRAY_SIZE(load_image_notify_routines))
        return STATUS_INSUFFICIENT_RESOURCES;

    load_image_notify_routines[load_image_notify_routine_count++] = routine;

    return STATUS_SUCCESS;

The table (ntoskrnl.c:73-74, 8 slots) is genuinely dispatched, but from exactly one place — the kernel-driver loader at ntoskrnl.c:4013-4034:

    if (module && load_image_notify_routine_count)
    ...
        info.ImageAddressingMode = IMAGE_ADDRESSING_MODE_32BIT;
        info.SystemModeImage = TRUE;
        ...
            load_image_notify_routines[i](&module_name, NULL, &info);

ProcessId is hardcoded NULL, SystemModeImage hardcoded TRUE, ImageAddressingMode hardcoded 32BIT.

The reference pattern for this whole audit — real work, honest failure code, TRACE not FIXME:

    TRACE("routine %p.\n", routine);

    for (i = 0; i < load_image_notify_routine_count; ++i)
        if (load_image_notify_routines[i] == routine)
        {
            --load_image_notify_routine_count;
            memmove(&load_image_notify_routines[i], &load_image_notify_routines[i + 1],
                    sizeof(*load_image_notify_routines) * (load_image_notify_routine_count - i));
            return STATUS_SUCCESS;
        }
    return STATUS_PROCEDURE_NOT_FOUND;

void WINAPI KeStackAttachProcess(KPROCESS *process, KAPC_STATE *apc_state)
{
    FIXME("process %p, apc_state %p stub.\n", process, apc_state);
}

No address-space switch, and apc_state is left uninitialized. Because the return type is void there is no status channel, so the caller has no way to learn it failed. A driver that then dereferences a target-process VA reads its *own* address space and gets plausible-looking garbage instead of a fault. That is a silent semantic lie, which for the plan's purposes is worse than a failure code.

void WINAPI KeUnstackDetachProcess(KAPC_STATE *apc_state)
{
    FIXME("apc_state %p stub.\n", apc_state);
}

Already honest — fails explicitly and zeroes the out-parameter:

    FIXME("fromprocess %p, fromaddress %p, toprocess %p, toaddress %p, bufsize %Iu, mode %d, copied %p stub.\n",
            fromprocess, fromaddress, toprocess, toaddress, bufsize, mode, copied);

    *copied = 0;
    return STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED;

Fabricates a physical address by reinterpreting the virtual one:

PHYSICAL_ADDRESS WINAPI MmGetPhysicalAddress(void *virtual_address)
{
    PHYSICAL_ADDRESS ret;
    FIXME("(%p): semi-stub\n", virtual_address);
    ret.QuadPart = (ULONG_PTR)virtual_address;
    return ret;
}

The returned value is not a physical address by any definition; it is the input pointer wearing a different type. A caller cannot distinguish it from a real answer.

Already honest — returns the documented failure value:

    FIXME( "stub: 0x%08lx%08lx, %ld, %ld\n", PhysicalAddress.HighPart, PhysicalAddress.LowPart, NumberOfBytes, CacheType );
    return NULL;

void WINAPI MmProbeAndLockPages(PMDLX MemoryDescriptorList, KPROCESSOR_MODE AccessMode, LOCK_OPERATION Operation)
{
    FIXME("(%p, %u, %u): stub\n", MemoryDescriptorList, AccessMode, Operation);
}

Neither half of the contract runs: no probe (so no exception is ever raised for an inaccessible buffer) and no lock (the MDL's PFN array is left untouched). Because the return type is void and the failure signal is an *exception*, doing nothing reads to the caller as unconditional success. Every driver's input validation built on this silently passes.

FIXME("(%p, %u, %u, %p, %lu, %u): stub\n", MemoryDescriptorList, AccessMode, CacheType, BaseAddress, BugCheckOnFailure, Priority);

    return NULL;

Honest failure. Contrast the plain MmMapLockedPages (ntoskrnl.c:2958-2965), which succeeds by returning mdl->StartVa + mdl->ByteOffset — defensible in Wine's single-address-space model and correct for the test at tests/driver.c:290-293.

A real implementation backed by wineserver, with explicit refusals for the parts it cannot do:

    if (mode != KernelMode)
    {
        FIXME( "UserMode access not implemented\n" );
        return STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED;
    }

    if (attr & ~OBJ_KERNEL_HANDLE) FIXME( "attr %#lx not supported\n", attr );
    if (access_state) FIXME( "access_state not implemented\n" );

    if (type && ObGetObjectType( obj ) != type) return STATUS_OBJECT_TYPE_MISMATCH;

    SERVER_START_REQ( get_kernel_object_handle )
    ...

The UserMode refusal is honest. The two bare FIXMEs are not: an access_state or an unsupported attr is logged and then *ignored*, and the function still returns STATUS_SUCCESS — so a caller that asked for a constrained open gets an unconstrained handle plus a success code.

NTSTATUS WINAPI ObReferenceObjectByPointer(void *obj, ACCESS_MASK access,
                                           POBJECT_TYPE type,
                                           KPROCESSOR_MODE mode)
{
    FIXME("(%p, %lx, %p, %d): stub\n", obj, access, type, mode);

    return STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED;
}

Honest. Noted because ObfReferenceObject immediately below it (ntoskrnl.c:3133) does work, so the two neighbours behave differently.

Honest, and defensively shaped — it nulls the out-parameter before failing:

NTSTATUS WINAPI SeLocateProcessImageName(PEPROCESS process, UNICODE_STRING **image_name)
{
    FIXME("stub: %p %p\n", process, image_name);
    if (image_name) *image_name = NULL;
    return STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED;
}

winebuild stubs; raise EXCEPTION_WINE_STUB when called. Related: KeBugCheckEx (ntoskrnl.c:3718) is

    ERR( "%lx %Ix %Ix %Ix %Ix\n", code, param1, param2, param3, param4 );
    ExitProcess( code );

Already honest:

NTSTATUS WINAPI CmRegisterCallback(EX_CALLBACK_FUNCTION *function, void *context, LARGE_INTEGER *cookie)
{
    FIXME("(%p %p %p): stub\n", function, context, cookie);
    return STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED;
}

Note it does not fabricate a cookie — compare ObRegisterCallbacks, which does.

Both are pure no-ops:

void WINAPI ProbeForRead(void *address, SIZE_T length, ULONG alignment)
{
    FIXME("(%p %Iu %lu) stub\n", address, length, alignment);
}

void WINAPI ProbeForWrite(void *address, SIZE_T length, ULONG alignment)
{
    FIXME("(%p %Iu %lu) stub\n", address, length, alignment);
}

The failure signal for these APIs is a raised exception, so "do nothing" is indistinguishable from "the buffer is valid". Every driver input-validation check built on them currently passes unconditionally.

Returns TRUE for both access modes without inspecting input or updating privilege attributes:

BOOLEAN WINAPI SePrivilegeCheck(PRIVILEGE_SET *privileges, SECURITY_SUBJECT_CONTEXT *context, KPROCESSOR_MODE mode)
{
    FIXME("stub: %p %p %u\n", privileges, context, mode);
    return TRUE;
}

The privilege set and the subject context are never examined.

BOOLEAN WINAPI SeSinglePrivilegeCheck(LUID privilege, KPROCESSOR_MODE mode)
{
    static int once;
    if (!once++) FIXME("stub: %08lx%08lx %u\n", privilege.HighPart, privilege.LowPart, mode);
    return TRUE;
}

The log is emitted once. KernelMode TRUE agrees with the documented boolean result; UserMode is returned TRUE without examining the current thread token.

NTSTATUS WINAPI ExCreateCallback(PCALLBACK_OBJECT *obj, POBJECT_ATTRIBUTES attr,
                                 BOOLEAN create, BOOLEAN allow_multiple)
{
    FIXME("(%p, %p, %u, %u): stub\n", obj, attr, create, allow_multiple);

    return STATUS_SUCCESS;
}

Worse than a fabricated handle: *obj is never written at all, so the caller proceeds with an uninitialized PCALLBACK_OBJECT and then hands it to ExRegisterCallback.

void * WINAPI ExRegisterCallback(PCALLBACK_OBJECT callback_object,
        PCALLBACK_FUNCTION callback_function, void *callback_context)
{
    FIXME("callback_object %p, callback_function %p, callback_context %p stub.\n",
            callback_object, callback_function, callback_context);

    return (void *)0xdeadbeef;
}

Fabricates a non-NULL registration pointer. NULL is the documented failure value, so this deliberately dodges it.

FIXME("provider %s, type %lu, enable_callback %p, context %p, handle %p\n", debugstr_guid(provider), type,
          callback, context, handle);

    *handle = 0xdeadbeef;
    return STATUS_SUCCESS;

Fabricates a REGHANDLE. EtwUnregister (ntoskrnl.c:4675) accepts it and returns STATUS_SUCCESS.

FIXME("(%u %lu %p %p %p %p %p) stub\n", category, flags, data, driver, callback, context, notification);
    return STATUS_SUCCESS;

Returns success without writing *notification, so the caller holds an uninitialized handle it will later pass to IoUnregisterPlugPlayNotification (ntoskrnl.c:3653), which also returns STATUS_SUCCESS.

BOOLEAN WINAPI KeInsertQueueDpc(PRKDPC Dpc, PVOID SystemArgument1, PVOID SystemArgument2)
{
    FIXME( "stub: (%p %p %p)\n", Dpc, SystemArgument1, SystemArgument2 );
    return TRUE;
}

TRUE means "the DPC was queued". It was not, and it will never run.

FIXME("(%p, %lu) stub\n", MemoryDescriptorList, NewProtect);
    return STATUS_SUCCESS;

Claims a memory protection change was applied when none was.

FIXME( "stub: %p\n", obj );
    return STATUS_SUCCESS;

No registration; the driver will never receive IRP_MJ_SHUTDOWN.

BOOLEAN WINAPI KeTestAlertThread(KPROCESSOR_MODE mode)
{
    FIXME("stub! %u\n", mode);
    return TRUE;
}

BOOLEAN WINAPI KeAlertThread(PKTHREAD thread, KPROCESSOR_MODE mode)
{
    FIXME("stub! %p mode %u\n", thread, mode);
    return TRUE;
}

Both report the alert state that suits the caller rather than the real one. These are the only two success-faking stubs in sync.c — the rest of that file is substantially real.

Wine traps the #GP from a privileged instruction a driver executes and synthesizes a control-register value. On x86_64:

        case 0x20: /* mov crX, Rd */
        ...
            switch (reg)
            {
            case 0: *data = 0x10; break; /* FIXME: set more bits ? */
            case 2: *data = 0; break;
            case 3: *data = 0; break;
            case 4: *data = 0; break;
            case 8: *data = 0; break;

0x10 is CR0_ET alone — CR0.PE and CR0.PG clear, which is not a state any 64-bit long-mode CPU can be in. The i386 path (instr.c:353) at least returns a self-consistent CR0_PE|CR0_ET|CR0_NE|CR0_WP|CR0_AM|CR0_PG. CR3 and CR4 are flat zero on both paths.

Honest — one of only five non-stub exports in ndis.sys, and it reports failure rather than faking a binding:

void WINAPI NdisRegisterProtocol(NDIS_STATUS *status, NDIS_HANDLE *handle,
                                 NDIS_PROTOCOL_CHARACTERISTICS *prot, UINT len)
{
    FIXME("(%p, %p, %p, %u): stub\n", status, handle, prot, len);
    *status = NDIS_STATUS_FAILURE;
}

Note it does not fabricate *handle. NdisAllocateMemoryWithTag (main.c:200) likewise returns NDIS_STATUS_FAILURE.
